I was having a tough time learning

265
00:08:00.239 --> 00:08:01.502
compared to the rest of my siblings.

266
00:08:01.502 --> 00:08:04.041
So, that was tough for me.

267
00:08:04.041 --> 00:08:06.303
But having that experience was just like

268
00:08:06.303 --> 00:08:07.578
the biggest culture shock for me.

269
00:08:07.578 --> 00:08:08.508
I remember everything vividly.

270
00:08:08.508 --> 00:08:09.341
Yeah.

271
00:08:09.341 --> 00:08:10.174
Yeah, at that age.

272
00:08:10.174 --> 00:08:11.007
Yeah.

273
00:08:11.007 --> 00:08:12.523
Well, to be going back and forth like that,

274
00:08:12.523 --> 00:08:15.437
probably gives you an added layer of perspective

275
00:08:15.437 --> 00:08:16.527
on the differences.

276
00:08:16.527 --> 00:08:17.367
Yeah, exactly.

277
00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:18.200
Yeah.

278
00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:19.033
Yes.

279
00:08:19.033 --> 00:08:21.153
But when you ultimately went back to begin high school,

280
00:08:21.153 --> 00:08:23.540
when you came to the United States, was that in Illinois?

281
00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.774
Is that where you kind of went to high school?

282
00:08:24.774 --> 00:08:25.789
Yeah, still the same town, DeKalb, Illinois.

283
00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:26.765
Same town, yeah.

284
00:08:26.765 --> 00:08:27.820
Right, right, right.

285
00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.888
And your parents are super accomplished.

286
00:08:29.888 --> 00:08:30.721
Yeah.

287
00:08:30.721 --> 00:08:31.560
I mean, they're highly educated.

288
00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.393
I know.

289
00:08:32.393 --> 00:08:33.326
How are they doing now?

290
00:08:33.326 --> 00:08:34.232
A lot of freshers.

291
00:08:34.232 --> 00:08:35.661
Yeah, both of them are retired now.

292
00:08:35.661 --> 00:08:38.885
And my mom was over 20-plus years as an English professor.

293
00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:39.839
Dad also.

294
00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:44.296
So, my dad has a PhD in the US, France, and England,

295
00:08:44.296 --> 00:08:46.775
and my mom has a PhD here and she has two masters.

296
00:08:46.775 --> 00:08:49.710
So, my older sister has a master.

297
00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:50.543
Wow.

298
00:08:50.543 --> 00:08:51.648
So, there's a lot of education.

299
00:08:51.648 --> 00:08:53.107
You're the broke wierdo.

300
00:08:53.107 --> 00:08:54.254
I literally I'm the weirdo of all.

301
00:08:54.254 --> 00:08:55.087
(Rich laughs)

302
00:08:55.087 --> 00:08:57.894
And younger brother, who's a mechanical engineer.

303
00:08:57.894 --> 00:09:00.277
And so, everybody, I was the only one actually

304
00:09:00.277 --> 00:09:03.607
that graduated high school at the normal age, at age 18,

305
00:09:03.607 --> 00:09:05.626
where they were all graduating at 16.

306
00:09:05.626 --> 00:09:06.459
Wow.

307
00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:07.292
Yeah.

308
00:09:07.292 --> 00:09:08.125
Wow.

309
00:09:08.125 --> 00:09:08.958
So, I was the athlete.

310
00:09:08.958 --> 00:09:09.791
Yeah.

311
00:09:09.791 --> 00:09:10.624
That's interesting.

312
00:09:10.624 --> 00:09:11.457
Yeah. (laughs)

313
00:09:11.457 --> 00:09:12.290
Cutting your own unique path.

314
00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.341
Exactly, yeah. Yeah.

315
00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:14.613
Which under that kind of pressure,

316
00:09:14.613 --> 00:09:16.659
I'm sure hasn't been easy.

317
00:09:16.659 --> 00:09:17.503
It wasn't easy at all.

318
00:09:17.503 --> 00:09:18.336
Yeah.

319
00:09:18.336 --> 00:09:19.169
For sure.

320
00:09:19.169 --> 00:09:20.002
But soccer was the thing.

321
00:09:20.002 --> 00:09:21.648
Soccer was always my dream.

322
00:09:21.648 --> 00:09:23.422
If you ask the 16-year-old Hellah,

323
00:09:23.422 --> 00:09:25.218
"Where would you be by age 25,"

324
00:09:25.218 --> 00:09:27.421
I had a goal in my mind to be playing in England

325
00:09:27.421 --> 00:09:28.681
for Chelsea FC.

326
00:09:28.681 --> 00:09:32.130
Chelsea was my team and soccer was all I wanted.

327
00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.921
And I realized toward the end of high school,

328
00:09:33.921 --> 00:09:35.358
that I can go to school for free.

329
00:09:35.358 --> 00:09:36.458
So, I started taking it serious,

330
00:09:36.458 --> 00:09:39.493
to a point where like my school grades was falling apart.

331
00:09:39.493 --> 00:09:41.523
And I came to the realization,

332
00:09:41.523 --> 00:09:42.996
"Hellah, you can be as good as you want in soccer,

333
00:09:42.996 --> 00:09:44.277
but if you don't take care of your grades,

334
00:09:44.277 --> 00:09:45.552
you're not gonna go to college."

335
00:09:45.552 --> 00:09:47.606
So, I started working well on both of it

336
00:09:47.606 --> 00:09:48.930
and I was able to get a scholarship

337
00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.610
to go to University of Massachusetts.

338
00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:51.443
Right.

339
00:09:51.443 --> 00:09:52.276
And you played there.

340
00:09:52.276 --> 00:09:53.109
Yeah.

341
00:09:53.109 --> 00:09:56.203
And ultimately took a stab at being a pro, right.

342
00:09:56.203 --> 00:09:57.036
Yes.

343
00:09:57.036 --> 00:09:58.053
Which had its kind of ups and downs.

344
00:09:58.053 --> 00:09:58.886
Yeah.

345
00:09:58.886 --> 00:10:02.287
That was a interesting story to me because for me,

346
00:10:02.287 --> 00:10:04.425
even to think that I could play professionally,

347
00:10:04.425 --> 00:10:06.554
just all happened by,

348
00:10:06.554 --> 00:10:09.775
in terms of a kind of a event that I didn't kinda plan

349
00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.074
for it to happen the way it did.

350
00:10:11.074 --> 00:10:14.210
So, I was playing for this team called Boston Victory.

351
00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:15.523
So, I said, PDL team, Premier Development

352
00:10:15.523 --> 00:10:17.256
or Professional Development League,

353
00:10:17.256 --> 00:10:18.511
where you can play at a high level

354
00:10:18.511 --> 00:10:20.213
and keep your college eligibility.

355
00:10:20.213 --> 00:10:22.492
So, we're playing against a team called Worcester Hydra

356
00:10:22.492 --> 00:10:24.209
in Worcester, Massachusetts.

357
00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:25.754
And I was Boston Victory.

358
00:10:25.754 --> 00:10:27.372
And there's a kid that was playing there,

359
00:10:27.372 --> 00:10:30.215
I'll never forget his name, Charlie Rug.

360
00:10:30.215 --> 00:10:31.793
Charlie played at Boston College.

361
00:10:31.793 --> 00:10:33.441
He was like ACC player of the year.

362
00:10:33.441 --> 00:10:34.941
Scored 20-something goals a season.

363
00:10:34.941 --> 00:10:36.227
So, my coach said, "Hey, Hellah,

364
00:10:36.227 --> 00:10:38.137
I'm gonna have you play left back

365
00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:39.213
because I know you have pace.

366
00:10:39.213 --> 00:10:41.770
You gotta keep up with this kid because he's really fast."

367
00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.567
So, I shut him down.

368
00:10:43.567 --> 00:10:44.582
Didn't go anywhere.

369
00:10:44.582 --> 00:10:46.533
I was up and down the flank.

370
00:10:46.533 --> 00:10:49.700
And had him even focused on defending me versus attacking.

371
00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:51.298
And if there's images of us,

372
00:10:51.298 --> 00:10:52.975
like literally I was glued to him.

373
00:10:52.975 --> 00:10:54.135
I didn't give him any space.

374
00:10:54.135 --> 00:10:56.019
But Sporting Kansas City had a scout

375
00:10:56.019 --> 00:10:59.806
sent to go watch that game, specifically for that kid.

376
00:10:59.806 --> 00:11:03.902
And after that, my little brother acting agent

377
00:11:03.902 --> 00:11:06.050
emailed some MLS teams and said,

378
00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.887
"I have a brother playing division one."

379
00:11:07.887 --> 00:11:09.638
So, Sporting Kansas City emailed me

380
00:11:09.638 --> 00:11:12.433
and said that, "We came to watch a game.

381
00:11:12.433 --> 00:11:14.636
And in fact, we wanted to look at someone else,

382
00:11:14.636 --> 00:11:15.767
but we're interested in you."

383
00:11:15.767 --> 00:11:17.023
Yeah, forget that other guy.

384
00:11:17.023 --> 00:11:18.125
What's going on with Hellah?

385
00:11:18.125 --> 00:11:20.425
"We're curious if you can play left midfield."

386
00:11:20.425 --> 00:11:21.744
And I was like, in fact, that is my position.

387
00:11:21.744 --> 00:11:23.922
I was only playing defense that day

388
00:11:23.922 --> 00:11:27.435
so I can shut down the player from BC.

389
00:11:27.435 --> 00:11:30.211
And that's when the interest of professional started for me.

390
00:11:30.211 --> 00:11:32.772
And I was like, wow, I can play in the MLS.

391
00:11:32.772 --> 00:11:33.605
That's incredible.

392
00:11:33.605 --> 00:11:34.438
Wow.

393
00:11:34.438 --> 00:11:35.271
Yeah.

394
00:11:35.271 --> 00:11:36.104
So, what happened in that?

395
00:11:36.104 --> 00:11:38.019
'Cause there were some like fits and starts.

396
00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:40.156
I mean, I've read a little bit about this,

397
00:11:40.156 --> 00:11:43.189
but it sounds like you were positioned to do really well,

398
00:11:43.189 --> 00:11:44.475
but there were visa problems

399
00:11:44.475 --> 00:11:46.603
and other kinds of things behind the scenes

400
00:11:46.603 --> 00:11:48.653
that really kind of prevented the trajectory

401
00:11:48.653 --> 00:11:49.913
going in the way that you wanted it to.

402
00:11:49.913 --> 00:11:51.889
Yes, so back then they had the super draft

403
00:11:51.889 --> 00:11:53.009
and the supplemented draft.

404
00:11:53.009 --> 00:11:55.652
So, super draft was like the first top rounds,

405
00:11:55.652 --> 00:11:57.763
and supplemented draft was the later rounds.

406
00:11:57.763 --> 00:11:58.596
And I was,

407
00:11:58.596 --> 00:12:00.701
the Sporting Kansas City emailed me and said that,

408
00:12:00.701 --> 00:12:02.174
"We're looking at you in the supplementary draft."

409
00:12:02.174 --> 00:12:04.056
So, which is like the later round.

410
00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:06.625
And then they said our concern is visa issues.

411
00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:07.889
So, the visa was the biggest problem.

412
00:12:07.889 --> 00:12:11.299
And to a point where they said that, "We can find someone

413
00:12:11.299 --> 00:12:14.132
as talented as you, or even not as talented as you,

414
00:12:14.132 --> 00:12:15.821
but we don't have to worry about visa issues

415
00:12:15.821 --> 00:12:18.362
so we can develop them to be where you're at."

416
00:12:18.362 --> 00:12:19.362
So, that was the factor.

417
00:12:19.362 --> 00:12:21.462
So, I got invited to this combine.

418
00:12:21.462 --> 00:12:22.812
And Sporting Kansas City was there.

419
00:12:22.812 --> 00:12:24.695
So many other MLS teams were there.

420
00:12:24.695 --> 00:12:27.502
And I was with this coach who was a head coach

421
00:12:27.502 --> 00:12:28.800
for Kitsap Pumas in Seattle,

422
00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.684
which is part of the Seattle Sounders.

423
00:12:30.684 --> 00:12:33.001
And he realized the visa issue

424
00:12:33.001 --> 00:12:35.115
and he wanted a Sporting Kansas City coaches

425
00:12:35.115 --> 00:12:37.197
and said, "Let me have Hellah for one year.

426
00:12:37.197 --> 00:12:38.606
And I'll even help him with the areas

427
00:12:38.606 --> 00:12:40.134
that you want him to improve.

428
00:12:40.134 --> 00:12:42.740
Hopefully within that year, we can sort out the visa stuff."

429
00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:45.320
And I signed my first professional contract in Seattle

430
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.231
playing with the Seattle Sounders affiliate.

431
00:12:47.231 --> 00:12:48.993
So, I was basically their foreign in team.

432
00:12:48.993 --> 00:12:51.256
We played against MLS teams.

433
00:12:51.256 --> 00:12:53.739
And actually, my first start, first game

434
00:12:53.739 --> 00:12:56.620
was against the Vancouver Whitecaps in Canada.

435
00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:57.988
And I got to get my first tested.

436
00:12:57.988 --> 00:13:00.325
I missed my graduation for that, which was cool.

437
00:13:00.325 --> 00:13:02.410
But the visa issue was the biggest for me.

438
00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:06.099
Yeah, it's interesting, because foreign nationals,

439
00:13:06.099 --> 00:13:07.390
there has to be a lot of foreign nationals

440
00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:09.644
who are kind of entering into the MLS system, right?

441
00:13:09.644 --> 00:13:10.477
Yeah.

442
00:13:10.477 --> 00:13:12.488
So, the immigration thing has to be something

443
00:13:12.488 --> 00:13:13.731
that they're dealing with all the time.

444
00:13:13.731 --> 00:13:14.564
Yeah.

445
00:13:14.564 --> 00:13:15.601
And there's a Roster cap.

446
00:13:15.601 --> 00:13:17.308
I don't know if it changed until this point,

447
00:13:17.308 --> 00:13:19.087
but in the national Roster space.

448
00:13:19.087 --> 00:13:20.915
So, out of like 22 to 26 players,

449
00:13:20.915 --> 00:13:22.828
only eight players can be foreign.

450
00:13:22.828 --> 00:13:24.197
And I have this message sent to me

451
00:13:24.197 --> 00:13:27.060
by a coach in Sporting Kansas City.

452
00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:28.366
He said, "Hellah, listen, I'm sorry,

453
00:13:28.366 --> 00:13:31.615
but we would rather have someone from Europe

454
00:13:31.615 --> 00:13:34.815
and that can sell jerseys pick up that spot

455
00:13:34.815 --> 00:13:36.394
versus someone new coming in."

456
00:13:36.394 --> 00:13:37.227
Wow.

457
00:13:37.227 --> 00:13:38.060
So, basically, it-

458
00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:38.893
It's like commercial reasons.

459
00:13:38.893 --> 00:13:40.021
It becomes even more of that.

460
00:13:40.021 --> 00:13:41.169
So, if you're a foreign national,

461
00:13:41.169 --> 00:13:42.221
you have to be extra time-

462
00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:43.197
Yeah, a big time.

463
00:13:43.197 --> 00:13:44.030
Yeah.

464
00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:45.388
Yeah, big time, and you get that spot.

465
00:13:45.388 --> 00:13:46.673
So, that got played out.

466
00:13:46.673 --> 00:13:49.614
I mean, how long were you part of that organization?

467
00:13:49.614 --> 00:13:50.921
For the team in Seattle?

468
00:13:50.921 --> 00:13:51.992
Yeah.

469
00:13:51.992 --> 00:13:52.895
Just the season.

470
00:13:52.895 --> 00:13:53.728
And right away, actually,

471
00:13:53.728 --> 00:13:55.272
I had my assistant coach from college,

472
00:13:55.272 --> 00:13:56.662
had a roommate in college

473
00:13:56.662 --> 00:13:59.024
when he was playing in college at Tampa University,

474
00:13:59.024 --> 00:14:01.303
Ray Finkle, who was an agent recruiting players

475
00:14:01.303 --> 00:14:02.136
to go to Europe.

476
00:14:02.136 --> 00:14:04.092
So, I had a chance to go play in Germany

477
00:14:04.092 --> 00:14:05.282
with the Bundesliga 2,

478
00:14:05.282 --> 00:14:07.319
which had some US national team players.

479
00:14:07.319 --> 00:14:08.341
So, nothing was guaranteed.

480
00:14:08.341 --> 00:14:09.578
Playing time wasn't guaranteed.

481
00:14:09.578 --> 00:14:12.147
But to be there for a certain amount of time was guaranteed.

482
00:14:12.147 --> 00:14:13.248
And I had to make my way up.

483
00:14:13.248 --> 00:14:16.078
So, I flew from Seattle, went to Boston

484
00:14:16.078 --> 00:14:18.066
and I went to the German embassy for my visa.

485
00:14:18.066 --> 00:14:19.082
They shut it.

486
00:14:19.082 --> 00:14:20.416
They said immediately,

487
00:14:20.416 --> 00:14:22.350
"We're concerned you're not a US citizen.

488
00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.404
Let's say you get to Germany, the season's over,

489
00:14:24.404 --> 00:14:27.034
your contract is done, are you gonna go back home?

490
00:14:27.034 --> 00:14:29.525
You're immigration threat in a way.

491
00:14:29.525 --> 00:14:30.822
You're probably not gonna go back to Africa.

492
00:14:30.822 --> 00:14:32.745
We don't want you to be an immigration issue in Germany."

493
00:14:32.745 --> 00:14:33.934
So, they shut that down.

494
00:14:33.934 --> 00:14:34.767
Wow.

495
00:14:34.767 --> 00:14:36.769
Yeah, I was sad about that because I was really-

496
00:14:36.769 --> 00:14:38.987
That has nothing to do with how good you are

497
00:14:38.987 --> 00:14:39.940
as a soccer player.

498
00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:42.185
No, it's something that's out of your control.

499
00:14:42.185 --> 00:14:43.495
And a friend of mine that got connected,

500
00:14:43.495 --> 00:14:45.445
well, he's a friend, we were connected.

501
00:14:45.445 --> 00:14:46.532
We were going together.

502
00:14:46.532 --> 00:14:47.365
He was a US citizen.

503
00:14:47.365 --> 00:14:49.118
A goalkeeper actually from California.

504
00:14:49.118 --> 00:14:51.612
He went there and I saw him living his dream

505
00:14:51.612 --> 00:14:53.685
playing in Europe, which is cool.

506
00:14:53.685 --> 00:14:55.002
But seeing that, I was like, "Ah, man,

507
00:14:55.002 --> 00:14:58.196
I could've almost been there, but visa also."

508
00:14:58.196 --> 00:15:00.076
So, you're a super positive guy.

509
00:15:00.076 --> 00:15:00.909
Yeah.

510
00:15:00.909 --> 00:15:01.742
That must have left you

511
00:15:01.742 --> 00:15:02.575
with a little bit of a bitter taste

512
00:15:02.575 --> 00:15:03.408
or something like that, right?

513
00:15:03.408 --> 00:15:04.241
A little bit, yeah.

514
00:15:04.241 --> 00:15:06.024
It got tough for me at that point.

515
00:15:06.024 --> 00:15:07.645
And you get to a point

516
00:15:07.645 --> 00:15:09.871
where you're like you start pointing fingers.

517
00:15:09.871 --> 00:15:12.227
And some of the excuses I was making back then

518
00:15:12.227 --> 00:15:13.535
were very valid.

519
00:15:13.535 --> 00:15:16.044
I got shut down to play in the youth national team

520
00:15:16.044 --> 00:15:17.215
in the US.

521
00:15:17.215 --> 00:15:18.768
I remember I was invited to go to Sarasota

522
00:15:18.768 --> 00:15:20.177
to play with a youth national team

523
00:15:20.177 --> 00:15:23.241
when I was playing with the Chicago Fire, Chicago Magic

524
00:15:23.241 --> 00:15:24.296
back in Illinois.

525
00:15:24.296 --> 00:15:26.138
And they said, "You gotta go tell your mom

526
00:15:26.138 --> 00:15:27.233
to get you your passport."

527
00:15:27.233 --> 00:15:28.066
I thought it was that easy.

528
00:15:28.066 --> 00:15:30.081
I went, "Mom, mom, give me my US passport."

529
00:15:30.081 --> 00:15:31.795
She's like, "Hellah, it's not that easy."

530
00:15:31.795 --> 00:15:34.239
So, all that start coming back into your mind

531
00:15:34.239 --> 00:15:35.913
and you feel like you have zero control.

532
00:15:35.913 --> 00:15:39.290
So, you get to a point where everything really bothers you.

533
00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.751
So, and then I was always a happy, exciting person.

534
00:15:41.751 --> 00:15:43.105
And that was really bothering me.

535
00:15:43.105 --> 00:15:45.026
At the time, I didn't know I was depressed,

536
00:15:45.026 --> 00:15:46.923
but if you were to ask me,

537
00:15:46.923 --> 00:15:48.765
then I would say to people, "Well, I'm just frustrated."

538
00:15:48.765 --> 00:15:50.521
So, there are things that never bothered me

539
00:15:50.521 --> 00:15:51.354
would start bothering me.

540
00:15:51.354 --> 00:15:52.342
And then now I look back,

541
00:15:52.342 --> 00:15:54.609
I think I was really depressed back then.

542
00:15:54.609 --> 00:15:55.885
And I just got to the realization,

543
00:15:55.885 --> 00:15:59.622
which is, with a run that made me feel like that

544
00:15:59.622 --> 00:16:00.641
to a point where I was like,

545
00:16:00.641 --> 00:16:01.896
"Hellah, what can you control now?

546
00:16:01.896 --> 00:16:04.143
What are you in control of in your life?"

547
00:16:04.143 --> 00:16:05.427
'Cause those things are not.

548
00:16:05.427 --> 00:16:08.385
So, that's when I started taking responsibility and say,

549
00:16:08.385 --> 00:16:11.050
"Go do what you can do, and focus on that.

550
00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:13.199
Put your energy there, not the areas you can't control."

551
00:16:13.199 --> 00:16:14.032
And what was that?

552
00:16:14.032 --> 00:16:15.335
I mean, when faced with the prospect

553
00:16:15.335 --> 00:16:18.949
of your professional career not panning out, what's next?

554
00:16:18.949 --> 00:16:21.196
I literally told myself one day,

555
00:16:21.196 --> 00:16:23.099
"I'm tired of making excuses.

556
00:16:23.099 --> 00:16:24.274
I gotta do something

557
00:16:24.274 --> 00:16:26.122
that I can hold myself accountable for."

558
00:16:26.122 --> 00:16:27.794
And then fitness comes to your mind.

559
00:16:27.794 --> 00:16:28.627
You're an athlete.

560
00:16:28.627 --> 00:16:29.953
And I said, "Hellah, whenever you tell yourself

561
00:16:29.953 --> 00:16:31.779
you're gonna hit the gym for a week straight,

562
00:16:31.779 --> 00:16:33.466
you go two, three days, you tap out."

563
00:16:33.466 --> 00:16:34.748
And I said, "What is something that I'm afraid of?"

564
00:16:34.748 --> 00:16:36.664
Running heavy immediately.

565
00:16:36.664 --> 00:16:38.941
So, playing division one, even at the pro level running,

566
00:16:38.941 --> 00:16:40.576
I was always afraid of it.

567
00:16:40.576 --> 00:16:43.006
At UMass Amherst, the track team used to look at us,

568
00:16:43.006 --> 00:16:43.936
"Are you guys the UMass track?

569
00:16:43.936 --> 00:16:45.214
You guys were on so much."

570
00:16:45.214 --> 00:16:46.813
Our coach was very proud of me.

571
00:16:46.813 --> 00:16:47.646
May he rest in peace.

572
00:16:47.646 --> 00:16:49.667
Sam Koch was very proud to say,

573
00:16:49.667 --> 00:16:50.949
"You guys are gonna be the fittest team in the country."

574
00:16:50.949 --> 00:16:53.533
So, anything you do, any mistake on the line,

575
00:16:53.533 --> 00:16:56.546
we want to play top teams, the day before, he makes us run.

576
00:16:56.546 --> 00:16:57.719
So, I was fitness test.

577
00:16:57.719 --> 00:16:58.552
I was afraid of it.

578
00:16:58.552 --> 00:17:00.778
I couldn't sleep the night before.

579
00:17:00.778 --> 00:17:01.611
I could play 90 minutes.

580
00:17:01.611 --> 00:17:04.417
No problem, because with soccer, you're heading 50/50 ball.

581
00:17:04.417 --> 00:17:05.425
You're attacking, you're defending.

582
00:17:05.425 --> 00:17:07.079
It's not just solely focused on running.

583
00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:08.912
So I told him, I asked myself, "What are you afraid of?"

584
00:17:08.912 --> 00:17:10.412
And running hit me.

585
00:17:10.412 --> 00:17:11.245
And I said, "You know what?

586
00:17:11.245 --> 00:17:12.301
I'm gonna go face my fear of running.

587
00:17:12.301 --> 00:17:15.297
Let me just go 10 minutes a day for two weeks

588
00:17:15.297 --> 00:17:16.331
and zero pressure."

589
00:17:16.331 --> 00:17:17.579
It doesn't matter if 10 minutes

590
00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:18.720
mean you don't even get to a mile.

591
00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:19.553
That's okay.

592
00:17:19.553 --> 00:17:20.409
So, I told myself that.

593
00:17:20.409 --> 00:17:22.690
Within the first week, I fell in love with it immediately.

594
00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.013
So, I ran to Alexa Torres, my fiancee.

595
00:17:25.013 --> 00:17:26.274
I was like, "I think I can do this for the rest of my life,

596
00:17:26.274 --> 00:17:28.124
but I don't wanna get ahead of myself.

597
00:17:28.124 --> 00:17:29.978
Let me do this every day for a year."

598
00:17:29.978 --> 00:17:31.491
So, that's how the whole run streak started.

599
00:17:31.491 --> 00:17:32.324
Right.

600
00:17:32.324 --> 00:17:34.425
So, it was really born out of this frustration

601
00:17:34.425 --> 00:17:35.697
and a little bit of confusion

602
00:17:35.697 --> 00:17:37.967
about what the next chapter was gonna look like.

603
00:17:37.967 --> 00:17:39.594
Yes. Exactly. Exactly.

604
00:17:39.594 --> 00:17:40.427
That's how it all started.

605
00:17:40.427 --> 00:17:43.066
It's also interesting that your frame on running

606
00:17:43.066 --> 00:17:44.873
is through the lens of punishment.

607
00:17:44.873 --> 00:17:46.123
Yeah. Uh-huh. (laughs)

608
00:17:46.123 --> 00:17:47.421
When you are on a sports team, it's not run-specific.

609
00:17:47.421 --> 00:17:50.135
It's like being told to run

610
00:17:50.135 --> 00:17:51.433
is what happens when you get in trouble?

611
00:17:51.433 --> 00:17:53.019
Literally. Yeah.

612
00:17:53.019 --> 00:17:55.050
It's like the stick.

613
00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:59.019
If it has two minutes of mine, I get so stressed

614
00:17:59.019 --> 00:18:00.353
the night before a fitness test.

615
00:18:00.353 --> 00:18:01.186
They can see it.

616
00:18:01.186 --> 00:18:02.019
They're like, "Hellah, are you okay?"

617
00:18:02.019 --> 00:18:03.377
I'm like, "I just can't stop thinking about that big test

618
00:18:03.377 --> 00:18:04.583
tomorrow morning." (laughs)

619
00:18:04.583 --> 00:18:05.416
(Rich laughs)

620
00:18:05.416 --> 00:18:06.556
Or like the corporate says,

621
00:18:06.556 --> 00:18:08.518
"Well, you gotta run two miles under 12 minutes."

622
00:18:08.518 --> 00:18:09.584
And if you don't make those times,

623
00:18:09.584 --> 00:18:10.956
you gotta be in breakfast club,

624
00:18:10.956 --> 00:18:13.489
which is extra fitness before the actual practice.

625
00:18:13.489 --> 00:18:15.844
And the actual practice has his own fitness in it.

626
00:18:15.844 --> 00:18:19.171
So, it really got me all confused and scared

627
00:18:19.171 --> 00:18:20.253
and petrified ultimately.

628
00:18:20.253 --> 00:18:21.190
But you're super fit.

629
00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:23.498
So, when you started this streak,

630
00:18:23.498 --> 00:18:24.788
I think you said you went out and ran 10 minutes

631
00:18:24.788 --> 00:18:26.063
or something like that.

632
00:18:26.063 --> 00:18:26.896
Yeah, it was 10 minutes a day.

633
00:18:26.896 --> 00:18:27.729
That was the goal.

634
00:18:27.729 --> 00:18:28.601
You're certainly capable of doing more than that.

635
00:18:28.601 --> 00:18:29.853
Yeah, way capable.

636
00:18:29.853 --> 00:18:31.131
And that 10 minutes didn't even last.

637
00:18:31.131 --> 00:18:33.104
It was just the first week I ended up finding myself

638
00:18:33.104 --> 00:18:34.275
running even four miles.

639
00:18:34.275 --> 00:18:36.490
But the whole point of that idea of saying 10 minutes a day

640
00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:38.391
was to have zero pressure.

641
00:18:38.391 --> 00:18:40.175
'Cause I know if I were to tell myself, "Hellah,

642
00:18:40.175 --> 00:18:41.961
you gotta run at least five miles a day,"

643
00:18:41.961 --> 00:18:44.115
that's something at that point in my mentality

644
00:18:44.115 --> 00:18:46.420
and physical ability for running,

645
00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:50.519
wasn't something I was kind of capable of maintaining,

646
00:18:50.519 --> 00:18:52.731
but I knew something attainable to do 10 minutes.

647
00:18:52.731 --> 00:18:54.036
So, it was a mental thing.

648
00:18:54.036 --> 00:18:55.109
So, that made me feel like,

649
00:18:55.109 --> 00:18:57.411
okay, you're doing this with non-pressure enjoying.

650
00:18:57.411 --> 00:19:00.277
Spring was in the air, it was May 15th of 2017.

651
00:19:00.277 --> 00:19:02.435
So, it was just a perfect amount of time

652
00:19:02.435 --> 00:19:03.324
for me to be out there.

653
00:19:03.324 --> 00:19:05.231
So, that's why I said 10 minutes.

654
00:19:05.231 --> 00:19:08.874
Streaks are so interesting psychologically, right?

655
00:19:08.874 --> 00:19:09.707
Yeah.

656
00:19:09.707 --> 00:19:10.720
I think it's really powerful

657
00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:13.879
to talk about setting a really low bar

658
00:19:13.879 --> 00:19:16.230
if you're contemplating something like that

659
00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:17.070
and making it doable.

660
00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:17.903
Yeah. Yes.

661
00:19:17.903 --> 00:19:20.665
So that you can actually learn to enjoy it

662
00:19:20.665 --> 00:19:23.072
before it becomes too challenging.

663
00:19:23.072 --> 00:19:23.905
Exactly.

664
00:19:23.905 --> 00:19:24.738
Where you're gonna pull the rip cord

665
00:19:24.738 --> 00:19:26.397
before you've even gotten out of the gate.

666
00:19:26.397 --> 00:19:27.230
Yeah.

667
00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:31.096
But once you develop a little bit of momentum,

668
00:19:31.096 --> 00:19:33.284
it becomes like this self-perpetuating thing, right?

669
00:19:33.284 --> 00:19:34.335
Yes, I agree.

670
00:19:34.335 --> 00:19:35.806
I don't know why that is.

671
00:19:35.806 --> 00:19:38.266
It's that thing, and I've said it many times before,

672
00:19:38.266 --> 00:19:41.614
when you're going to the gym consistently,

673
00:19:41.614 --> 00:19:42.902
it's just easier to go.

674
00:19:42.902 --> 00:19:44.075
Or if you're running every day,

675
00:19:44.075 --> 00:19:45.119
it's easier to get out and run.

676
00:19:45.119 --> 00:19:46.127
And get it done.

677
00:19:46.127 --> 00:19:49.136
But if you break that cycle, if you interrupt it,

678
00:19:49.136 --> 00:19:50.031
even once-

679
00:19:50.031 --> 00:19:50.976
Oh, man. (laughs)

680
00:19:50.976 --> 00:19:53.324
And you just create all kinds of anxiety

681
00:19:53.324 --> 00:19:54.807
and strain on yourself.
Everything.

682
00:19:54.807 --> 00:19:55.938
That makes it so difficult.

683
00:19:55.938 --> 00:19:58.701
Yeah, the starting over thinking and all that.

684
00:19:58.701 --> 00:20:00.248
So, it's very true.

685
00:20:00.248 --> 00:20:01.418
Exactly how you said it.

686
00:20:01.418 --> 00:20:02.251
Right.

687
00:20:02.251 --> 00:20:03.507
So when you first began this,

688
00:20:03.507 --> 00:20:05.355
was the idea of a streak even in your mind,

689
00:20:05.355 --> 00:20:07.085
or you were just like, "I'm gonna go out and run

690
00:20:07.085 --> 00:20:09.657
'cause I want to be able to have some control over myself."

691
00:20:09.657 --> 00:20:10.971
It wasn't even as streak.

692
00:20:10.971 --> 00:20:13.273
It was just two weeks that I needed to do.

693
00:20:13.273 --> 00:20:15.901
And the thing is, to my knowledge,

694
00:20:15.901 --> 00:20:17.806
I didn't know what streaking actually was for running.

695
00:20:17.806 --> 00:20:19.599
I didn't even know it was a thing in the running community.

696
00:20:19.599 --> 00:20:21.305
I was so new to the running community.

697
00:20:21.305 --> 00:20:22.167
But just for the record,

698
00:20:22.167 --> 00:20:24.061
we're not talking about running naked. (laughs)

699
00:20:24.061 --> 00:20:26.738
(laughs) Yes, we're not really.

700
00:20:26.738 --> 00:20:28.719
We're talking about running consecutively so well.

701
00:20:28.719 --> 00:20:29.552
Consecutively, yes.

702
00:20:29.552 --> 00:20:30.385
Yeah, that's a good point.

703
00:20:30.385 --> 00:20:31.218
That's about it. (laughs)

704
00:20:31.218 --> 00:20:32.051
(Rich laughs)

705
00:20:32.051 --> 00:20:35.584
Yeah, so, I was very ignorant to it and didn't know.

706
00:20:35.584 --> 00:20:36.794
And that was really good.

707
00:20:36.794 --> 00:20:37.974
I'm glad I didn't know about it

708
00:20:37.974 --> 00:20:40.429
because also that would have been, "Oh, people streak?"

709
00:20:40.429 --> 00:20:42.473
That's another pressure that I didn't need.

710
00:20:42.473 --> 00:20:43.381
So, I didn't even know.

711
00:20:43.381 --> 00:20:44.667
And that it was just to get out there.

712
00:20:44.667 --> 00:20:48.040
And I just knew I wanted to do it every day now for a year.

713
00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:49.809
Is there a whole world of people

714
00:20:49.809 --> 00:20:51.883
who do these streaks out there and document them?

715
00:20:51.883 --> 00:20:52.996
I discovered...

716
00:20:52.996 --> 00:20:53.829
Not document.

717
00:20:53.829 --> 00:20:56.746
I think humbly we became hellagood,

718
00:20:58.384 --> 00:21:00.551
like my YouTube channel and with my fiancee,

719
00:21:00.551 --> 00:21:01.808
'cause it's a team,

720
00:21:01.808 --> 00:21:04.059
we became basically the first people

721
00:21:04.059 --> 00:21:06.985
to really put it on the platform of running every day

722
00:21:06.985 --> 00:21:09.280
and running every day for a year, 365 days.

723
00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.370
And then now everybody that won't do it, started doing it.

724
00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:13.665
Or everybody that were actually been doing it,

725
00:21:13.665 --> 00:21:16.016
started documenting it and push it out.

726
00:21:16.016 --> 00:21:17.549
And which I'm so happy about

727
00:21:17.549 --> 00:21:20.243
because I actually am happier for more people

728
00:21:20.243 --> 00:21:22.282
who are doing the streak compared to me,

729
00:21:22.282 --> 00:21:25.290
because a lot of questions comes, "Oh, he's the lucky one.

730
00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:26.123
How is he able to do it?

731
00:21:26.123 --> 00:21:27.267
But when I see someone else, then I'm like,

732
00:21:27.267 --> 00:21:28.438
"See, it's not just me.

733
00:21:28.438 --> 00:21:29.550
Look at this person did it.

734
00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:30.383
That person did it."

735
00:21:30.383 --> 00:21:31.216
So-

736
00:21:31.216 --> 00:21:35.257
When you made your 365 day, your one-year streak video,

737
00:21:35.257 --> 00:21:38.601
most of that video was clips of other people out running

738
00:21:38.601 --> 00:21:39.597
in different parts of the world.

739
00:21:39.597 --> 00:21:40.430
Exactly.

740
00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:42.982
Because I realized how much running brought us together.

741
00:21:42.982 --> 00:21:44.861
And we started building a community.

742
00:21:44.861 --> 00:21:46.581
And I wanted to show that to people

743
00:21:46.581 --> 00:21:48.840
and talk about my story of how running every day

744
00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.948
changed my life and how people became a part of it.

745
00:21:51.948 --> 00:21:53.266
So, to me, it's all about the people.

746
00:21:53.266 --> 00:21:55.033
I think people make the experience.

747
00:21:55.033 --> 00:21:56.319
You could do a lot of stuff by yourself,

748
00:21:56.319 --> 00:21:59.002
but without the people, I feel like it's not the same.

749
00:21:59.002 --> 00:21:59.835
Right. Right.

750
00:21:59.835 --> 00:22:02.754
Well, at first it becomes this journey of self-discovery

751
00:22:02.754 --> 00:22:06.292
and an exploration of your own personal existential.

752
00:22:06.292 --> 00:22:07.125
Exactly.

753
00:22:07.125 --> 00:22:09.012
But at some point, it becomes rote

754
00:22:09.012 --> 00:22:11.134
and it has to become about something else

755
00:22:11.134 --> 00:22:12.251
in order to maintain its meaningfulness.

756
00:22:12.251 --> 00:22:15.170
Yes, I agree with you a 100%, yeah.

757
00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:17.011
So, you start this thing off.

758
00:22:17.011 --> 00:22:18.305
You're you're doing it.

759
00:22:18.305 --> 00:22:19.934
You're not sharing anything about it

760
00:22:19.934 --> 00:22:21.199
until you reach a certain point.

761
00:22:21.199 --> 00:22:22.380
Yeah.

762
00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.912
And then you decide to kind of break public

763
00:22:24.912 --> 00:22:25.745
in this whole thing.

764
00:22:25.745 --> 00:22:28.446
Yeah, actually, that's a good point to bring up,

765
00:22:28.446 --> 00:22:31.379
because for me, this was not meant for social media.

766
00:22:31.379 --> 00:22:32.686
It was just for myself.

767
00:22:32.686 --> 00:22:35.936
And it was Lex Torres Belle, my fiance,

768
00:22:37.085 --> 00:22:38.361
when I started this running streak, she was like,

769
00:22:38.361 --> 00:22:39.805
"I think we gotta do a YouTube video.

770
00:22:39.805 --> 00:22:40.987
This is really cool.

771
00:22:40.987 --> 00:22:42.620
You're running everyday and you're going for a year.

772
00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:43.527
People need to hear about this."

773
00:22:43.527 --> 00:22:44.808
I keep pushing it off.

774
00:22:44.808 --> 00:22:45.984
I was always pushing it out.

775
00:22:45.984 --> 00:22:48.740
So, on day 163, she came to me and she said,

776
00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:50.228
"Hey, do you wanna do a YouTube video on this?"

777
00:22:50.228 --> 00:22:51.061
I was like, "All right, fine.

778
00:22:51.061 --> 00:22:52.603
You keep asking, let's just do it."

779
00:22:52.603 --> 00:22:54.804
I had just done my run for the day.

780
00:22:54.804 --> 00:22:56.225
So I said, "Can I just go to the barbershop

781
00:22:56.225 --> 00:22:58.611
and get my haircut and get this like nice face,

782
00:22:58.611 --> 00:23:00.431
so I can look pretty in front of the camera?"

783
00:23:00.431 --> 00:23:03.072
And then I went to get my haircut, came back.

784
00:23:03.072 --> 00:23:03.905
We did a video.

785
00:23:03.905 --> 00:23:07.070
And the title was "Why I Run Every Day: Day 163."

786
00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:08.675
And I started explaining to people.

787
00:23:08.675 --> 00:23:11.535
So, that video actually sat on YouTube for a month or two.

788
00:23:11.535 --> 00:23:14.483
It wasn't a lot of views, couple, maybe 20, 30 views.

789
00:23:14.483 --> 00:23:17.495
So, one morning I woke up, it was like 2000 views overnight.

790
00:23:17.495 --> 00:23:19.279
And I was like, "Belle, look at this.

791
00:23:19.279 --> 00:23:20.112
This is really weird.

792
00:23:20.112 --> 00:23:21.317
Why is this catching on like this?"

793
00:23:21.317 --> 00:23:23.015
And then we see 20,000 views overnight.

794
00:23:23.015 --> 00:23:25.441
